            <ArticleTitle>Correlative Single-Molecule Localization Microscopy and Confocal Microscopy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>205-217</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/978-1-4939-7265-4_17</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Single-molecule localization microscopy allows the ability to image fluorescence labeled molecular targets at nanoscale resolution. However, for many biological questions the ability to provide tissue and cellular context in addition to these high resolution data is eminently informative. Here, we describe a procedure to achieve this aim by correlatively imaging human cardiac tissue first at the nanoscale with direct stochastic optical reconstruction microscopy (dSTORM) and then at the diffraction limit with conventional confocal microscopy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
